<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">brjcancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">2803919</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2247361</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Yttrium-90-EDTMP: a radiotherapeutic agent in the treatment of leukaemias.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Keeling</surname><given-names>A. A.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Vaughan</surname><given-names>A. T.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Beaney</surname><given-names>R. P.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>Department of Immunology, University of Birmingham, UK.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>7</month><year>1989</year></pub-date><volume>60</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>74</fpage><lpage>78</lpage><abstract><p>Yttrium-90 chelated by the tetraphosphonate EDTMP achieved a high uptake in bone and a rapid clearance from all soft tissues compared with 90Y nitrilotriacetate, citrate and acetate. The biological half-life of 90Y in the bone was greater than 72 h, but the quantity, and therefore dose, could be reduced by 50% using repeated, non-toxic chelation therapy with the calcium salt of DTPA. This treatment should be able to supplement current treatments for leukaemia where the dose of external beam radiation is associated with considerable morbidity.</p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><supplementary-material content-type="scanned-pages"><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00117-0084.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="74" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00117-0085.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="75" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00117-0086.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="76" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00117-0087.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="77" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/><graphic xlink:href="brjcancer00117-0088.tif" xlink:title="scanned-page" xlink:role="78" mimetype="image" mime-subtype="tiff"/></supplementary-material></body></article>